### Timeline Extended: the mod's own window, first for the systems with no
### vanilla home (docs/systems/system_panels_feasibility.md §9, item 3), and
### since 2026-11-02 for every system (style guide rule 9). Three strips of
### five: Nation (what the country carries for good, always open;
### gui/te_nation_tab.gui), the Space Race, Grand Monuments, the Colonial
### Empire and the Seabed (Global Commons); then the tabs that also live in
### the Budget and Market panels (Banking, Tax Code, Budget Breakdown,
### Strategic Reserve, Global Warming); then those of the Population, Society,
### Diplomacy and Military panels (Demographics, Cultural Hegemony, United
### Nations, Nuclear Weapons, Covert Warfare), each the same body type as its
### host's. Additive: a scripted widget
### (gui/scripted_widgets/te_systems_window.txt registers the two roots at the
### foot of this file), so no vanilla file is replaced.
###
### WHAT LIVES WHERE (spec docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-02-te-window-wide-mode-design.md
### § 9.3). This file keeps the two frames (te_systems_window_panel, the side
### panel, and te_systems_window_panel_wide, the full screen), the width
### toggle, Tax Code's footer, the launcher and the root. The strips, the
### selection templates and each tab's mount
### are generated into gui/te_systems_window_generated.gui from the TABS
### registry in scripts/generators/gen_te_systems_window.py: never edit that
### file, edit the registry and run the script. Each tab's content is a body
### type in its system's file (te_<system>_tab_body, on te_system_tab_frame in
### gui/te_system_tab_widgets.gui; Nation's is te_nation_tab_sections, Budget
### Breakdown's te_budget_breakdown_panel, Demographics' te_demog_window_body),
### the one a vanilla host's tab mounts too. To add
### a tab: its body type, a Tab line in the registry, its loc, then run the
### generator.
###
### te_systems_window_launcher  a small button under the sidebar's Map List,
###                             built as vanilla builds Map List
###                             (information_panel_bar.gui)
### te_systems_window           the window: vanilla's default_block_window
###                             where vanilla's side panels sit, or its
###                             fullscreen_block_window copy at the origin
###
### STATE. All three are client-side GetVariableSystem values (never saved):
###   com_open_window = te_systems_window   the window is open. The key is the
###       Community Mod Framework's (system_panels_feasibility.md §7.5, item 3):
###       with CMF enabled, its own clearing closes this window along with its
###       own; without CMF nothing else reads the key.
###   te_systems_window_wide = yes   the window is full screen (the toggle in
###       each frame's fixed_top); unset, it is a side panel. A separate key,
###       so the chosen tab survives the switch.
###   te_systems_window_tab = the TABS registry's keys (nation, space_race, …,
###       covert_warfare)
###       the tab the player last chose. The tab shown is that one while it is
###       open, otherwise Nation, which always is (the generated
###       te_systems_window_*_selected templates; the rule is in the generator's
###       docstring, and test_te_systems_window.py checks them per stored value).
### Set from outside this file too: the state view's Grand Monument card
### (te_state_gm_card in te_state_panel_widgets.gui) sets the tab to
### grand_monuments, then opens the window. Renaming either value breaks it
### (StateCardTest in test_grand_monuments_layout.py).
###
### PANEL STACK. A scripted widget is not in the engine's panel stack, so the
### window does it by hand (§3.6): opening it closes any vanilla side panel and
### the ledger (the root's _show), and a vanilla panel or the ledger opening
### closes it (the two trigger_when widgets in the launcher's root, which is up
### whenever the window can be).
###
### GATES, outermost first, so nothing inside a closed one is evaluated:
###   the played country (GetMetaPlayer.GetPlayedOrObservedCountry.IsValid, and
###   not observer mode, where GetPlayer is empty); then, for the window, its open
###   state on the root and each tab's content only while that tab is shown
###   (§8 test 3, whether a hidden scripted widget stops updating, is untested);
###   then per system the scope-free gate on a parent and the
###   GetPlayerJournalEntry datacontext on a child (gotchas #22 and #29 in
###   docs/guides/gui_modding_guide.md). Gates: te_window_* in
###   common/scripted_guis/te_system_tab_sguis.txt for the window's own
###   systems, the host tabs' own (te_budget_*, te_market_*, …) for the rest.
###
### The launcher's icon: docs/systems/te_systems_window_gui_icons.md. The tabs
### carry none.

### The tabs' selection templates, strips and mounts are generated:
### gui/te_systems_window_generated.gui, from the TABS registry in
### scripts/generators/gen_te_systems_window.py, whose docstring has the rule
### for which tab is shown.

types te_systems_window_types
{
	### The window: vanilla's side-panel frame (block_windows.gui:317), 540
	### wide, titled "Timeline Extended", with the tab strip in fixed_top.
	type te_systems_window_panel = default_block_window {
		name = "te_systems_window_panel"

		blockoverride "window_header_name" {
			text = "te_systems_window_title"
		}

		### Back, as on every vanilla panel: the header art keeps a socket for
		### the button, so an emptied block left a hole (play-test 2026-09-30).
		### It closes the window. Vanilla's InformationPanelBar.OpenPreviousPanel
		### was tried and reopened nothing in game: the window closes the panel
		### it replaces with ClosePanel, which leaves no history.
		blockoverride "header_back_button" {
			onclick = "[GetVariableSystem.Clear('com_open_window')]"
			input_action = "back"
		}

		### The close button clears the open state. default_block_window puts
		### shortcut = "close_window" on the button outside this block, so
		### Escape closes the window too.
		blockoverride "header_close_button" {
			onclick = "[GetVariableSystem.Clear('com_open_window')]"
		}

		### The strips (te_systems_window_strips_narrow, generated): vanilla's
		### strip, as the Military panel uses it (panel_military.gui).
		### Each tab is on the strip while its system's rule is on, greyed
		### until one of its entries runs; the greyed tab's tooltip lists the
		### unlock conditions with a tick or a cross. When they all pass but the
		### entry is not running (in a save whose entry had run its course, the
		### checklist came out empty: play-test 2026-09-30), the tooltip says so
		### instead (te_system_tab_met_tt). tab_buttons defaults both
		### halves of a slot to `visible = no`, so each visibility block says
		### when its half shows: the generated templates.
		###
		### No tab carries an icon, as vanilla's tabs and the mod's other tabs
		### don't (owner, 2026-09-30), so each tab shows the system's full name.
		### Each strip holds five, as vanilla's Diplomacy panel puts its five
		### (diplomatic_overview.gui: Overview, Subjects, Release Subject, Nation
		### Formation, Countries) on plain tab_buttons in this same 540 window,
		### with no size or height override. A slot is 104.8 wide ((540 - 2 x 3
		### margin - 2 x 5 dividers) / 5), so a name gets 84.8 inside its 10 px
		### margins. "Colonial Empire" and "Grand Monuments" are wider than that
		### on one line at EB Garamond 17, as vanilla's "Release Subject" and
		### "Nation Formation" are; vanilla's tab_text_properties wraps a name
		### (multiline) and shrinks it down to fontsize_min = 12, so the two
		### words stack. "Monuments" is no longer than vanilla's "Formation".
		### Until 2026-10-17 the Colonial Empire and the Seabed sat on a second
		### strip so the names kept one line (owner: two levels for five tabs
		### was a bit silly when the other windows hold up to six on one).
		blockoverride "fixed_top" {
			vbox = {
				layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
				layoutpolicy_vertical = fixed
				ignoreinvisible = yes

				te_systems_window_toggle = {
					blockoverride "toggle" {
						text = "te_systems_window_wide_on"
						tooltip = "te_systems_window_wide_on_tt"
						onclick = "[GetVariableSystem.Set('te_systems_window_wide', 'yes')]"
					}
				}
				te_systems_window_strips_narrow = {}
			}
		}

		### One fixed-width column (style guide rule 8), holding each tab's
		### mount in the strip's order, each up only while its tab is shown.
		### Each tab's content is a body type in its system's file
		### (te_<system>_tab_body, on te_system_tab_frame in
		### te_system_tab_widgets.gui; Nation's is te_nation_tab_sections).
		blockoverride "scrollarea_content" {
			te_systems_window_tabs_narrow = {}
		}

		blockoverride "fixed_bottom" {
			te_systems_window_tax_footer = {}
		}
	}

	### The full-screen frame (spec 2026-11-02-te-window-wide-mode § 4):
	### te_fullscreen_block_window, vanilla's fullscreen_block_window with Back
	### and Close clearing com_open_window (generated from vanilla's file; see
	### test_te_fullscreen_frame.py), titled as the side panel is. Its fixed_top
	### holds the same toggle, at the left (clear of the outliner, see the
	### toggle's type), then the strips side by side; its column holds each
	### tab's wide mount.
	type te_systems_window_panel_wide = te_fullscreen_block_window {
		name = "te_systems_window_panel_wide"

		blockoverride "window_header_name" {
			text = "te_systems_window_title"
		}

		blockoverride "fixed_top" {
			vbox = {
				layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
				layoutpolicy_vertical = fixed
				ignoreinvisible = yes

				te_systems_window_toggle = {
					blockoverride "toggle_lead" {}
					blockoverride "toggle_trail" {
						expand = {}
					}
					blockoverride "toggle" {
						text = "te_systems_window_wide_off"
						tooltip = "te_systems_window_wide_off_tt"
						onclick = "[GetVariableSystem.Clear('te_systems_window_wide')]"
					}
				}
				te_systems_window_strips_wide = {}
			}
		}

		blockoverride "scrollarea_content" {
			te_systems_window_tabs_wide = {}
		}

		blockoverride "fixed_bottom" {
			te_systems_window_tax_footer = {}
		}
	}

	### Tax Code's footer, in both frames' fixed_bottom (plan C9): the Budget
	### panel's (gui/budget_panel.gui), the draft summary (te_tax_draft_summary,
	### te_tax_workbench_widget.gui) or, with no draft open, the bill under
	### debate's (te_tax_bill_summary, te_tax_politics_widget.gui: its support,
	### its debate, Pass, Force Through and Withdraw), below the scrolling
	### content so their buttons stay on screen while the player works through
	### the workbench. Each summary gates itself. Only on the Tax Code tab (its
	### selected template, which carries the tab's open gate) and only while a
	### draft or a bill is; the tab's body leaves the draft's own copy out
	### (te_tax_tab_body's "draft_summary"). The summaries read GetPlayer only.
	### Budget's band at Budget's width in both frames: a flowcontainer can't
	### stretch to the full-screen frame, and a fixed 1,620 would overflow a
	### 1600 wide screen.
	type te_systems_window_tax_footer = flowcontainer {
		using = te_systems_window_tax_code_selected
		direction = vertical
		### No parentanchor: both frames' fixed_bottom is a vbox child, where a
		### position is ignored and logs "Widget cannot have a position in a
		### layout" each time the footer is laid out (gotcha #47). Budget's
		### footer, on the same frame, has none either.
		ignoreinvisible = yes

		flowcontainer = {
			visible = "[Or( GetScriptedGui('te_tax_show_draft_sgui').IsShown( GuiScope.SetRoot( GetPlayer.MakeScope ).End ), GetScriptedGui('te_tax_show_bill_sgui').IsShown( GuiScope.SetRoot( GetPlayer.MakeScope ).End ) )]"
			direction = vertical
			parentanchor = hcenter
			margin_bottom = 4
			ignoreinvisible = yes

			divider_clean = {
				size = { 554 4 }
			}

			background = {
				using = dark_area
			}

			te_tax_draft_summary = {}
			te_tax_bill_summary = {}
		}
	}

	### The width toggle (spec § 4.2): a slim row first in each frame's
	### fixed_top, a small text button. The frames expose no header slot for
	### it. It writes only te_systems_window_wide, so the chosen tab survives
	### the switch. An icon can replace the text later (style guide rule 10).
	###
	### At the row's right by default (the side panel). The full-screen frame
	### moves it to the left ("toggle_lead" empty, "toggle_trail" the expand):
	### vanilla's outliner sits on the HUD layer, above this window, over the
	### screen's right 360 px from y = 155 (ingame_hud.gui), and a covered Side
	### Panel would have left the window full screen for the session. Since
	### 2026-11-02 the outliner hides while the full-screen frame draws, as
	### under vanilla's full screens (gui/00_te_fullscreen_hide.gui); the
	### toggle stays at the left all the same.
	type te_systems_window_toggle = hbox {
		layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
		layoutpolicy_vertical = fixed
		margin = { 10 0 }

		block "toggle_lead" {
			expand = {}
		}

		button = {
			using = default_button
			size = { 140 26 }
			block "toggle" {}
		}

		block "toggle_trail" {}
	}
}

### ==========================================================================
### THE LAUNCHER (registered: te_systems_window_launcher). The next slot under
### Map List, as vanilla places its small buttons: the sidebar sits at
### { 0 200 }, its buttons' flowcontainer has a 5 px top margin, and Map List is
### the 40-high slot at +580, so the next is at 200 + 5 + 620 = 825. The button
### is vanilla's own (sidepanel_button_small, lit by
### selected_sidepanel_animation_small while the window is open), with vanilla's
### tooltip area over it.
###
### THE HOVER LABEL, built as vanilla builds the sidebar's (information_panel_bar.gui).
### Vanilla's flowcontainer of label buttons runs
### PdxGuiInterruptThenTriggerAllAnimations on onmousehierarchyenter / -leave
### ('hide_sidebar_labels' then 'show_sidebar_labels', and the reverse), which
### slides each label out (its own states, in vanilla's sidebar_label_button_small
### and sidebar_label_text_small types) and fades the dark strip behind them in
### (the first widget below, a copy of the bar's own). The launcher does the same
### with its own flowcontainer, the same state names and vanilla's two label
### types, so it slides out on the hover of its own button. Whether the call also
### reaches the sidebar's widgets, so that hovering either slides out both, is
### untested: PdxGuiInterruptThenTriggerAllAnimations is not documented. The strip
### has no start_sound: if the call is global, the sidebar's own would play twice.
###
### The label is two buttons, one per state, as vanilla's power bloc pair is:
### the closed one opens the window and the open one closes it.
### ==========================================================================
widget = {
	name = "te_systems_window_launcher"
	layer = top
	parentanchor = top|left
	position = { 0 825 }
	size = { 42 40 }
	allow_outside = yes
	visible = "[And( GetMetaPlayer.GetPlayedOrObservedCountry.IsValid, Not( GetMetaPlayer.IsObserver ) )]"

	### The panel stack, as the Community Mod Framework does it
	### (com_gui_sidebar.gui): a vanilla side panel or the ledger opening
	### closes the window. Here, not in the window, so each fires on the moment
	### the panel opens, never on the window's own first frame.
	widget = {
		state = {
			trigger_when = "[InformationPanelBar.IsAnyPanelOpen]"
			on_finish = "[GetVariableSystem.Clear('com_open_window')]"
		}
	}
	widget = {
		state = {
			trigger_when = "[MapListPanelManager.IsVisible]"
			on_finish = "[GetVariableSystem.Clear('com_open_window')]"
		}
	}

	### Hidden on the pause and game-over screens, as the sidebar is.
	widget = {
		size = { 100% 100% }
		using = hud_visibility

		widget = {
			name = "te_systems_window_launcher_button"
			size = { 100% 100% }

			### The dark strip behind the label: vanilla's, sized for one row
			### (the bar's is as tall as the whole sidebar). Its right margin
			### (+40) extends the strip past the widget, so it ends at about
			### width + 40. 200 puts that where vanilla's dark area ends, near
			### 240 (measured from a play-test screenshot, 2026-09-30: 300 ran
			### about 100 past it).
			widget = {
				size = { 200 40 }
				alpha = 0

				background = {
					using = dark_area
					alpha = 1
					margin_right = 40
					margin_top = -5
					margin_left = -25
					margin_bottom = -5

					modify_texture = {
						texture = "gfx/interface/masks/fade_horizontal_right.dds"
						spriteType = Corneredstretched
						spriteborder = { 0 0 }
						blend_mode = alphamultiply
					}
				}

				state = {
					name = show_sidebar_labels
					alpha = 0.7
					duration = 0.15
					using = Animation_Curve_Default
				}
				state = {
					name = hide_sidebar_labels
					alpha = 0
					duration = 0.15
					using = Animation_Curve_Default
				}
			}

			flowcontainer = {
				name = "te_systems_window_launcher_hover"
				margin_right = -8 # trims the hover area for the label, as vanilla's does
				onmousehierarchyenter = "[PdxGuiInterruptThenTriggerAllAnimations('hide_sidebar_labels','show_sidebar_labels')]"
				onmousehierarchyleave = "[PdxGuiInterruptThenTriggerAllAnimations('show_sidebar_labels','hide_sidebar_labels')]"

				container = {
					### The label: closed opens the window, open closes it.
					sidebar_label_button_small = {
						visible = "[Not( GetVariableSystem.HasValue('com_open_window', 'te_systems_window') )]"
						position = { -100 0 }
						blockoverride "onclick" {
							onclick = "[GetVariableSystem.Set('com_open_window', 'te_systems_window')]"
						}
					}
					sidebar_label_button_small = {
						visible = "[GetVariableSystem.HasValue('com_open_window', 'te_systems_window')]"
						position = { -100 0 }
						blockoverride "onclick" {
							onclick = "[GetVariableSystem.Clear('com_open_window')]"
						}
					}
					sidebar_label_text_small = {
						position = { -100 0 }
						text = "te_systems_window_launcher_label"
					}

					### Closed: opens the window.
					widget = {
						size = { 42 40 }
						visible = "[Not( GetVariableSystem.HasValue('com_open_window', 'te_systems_window') )]"

						widget = {
							using = clickthrough_blocker
							using = sidebar_button_size_small
							using = selected_sidepanel_animation_small
							blockoverride "selected_visibility" {
								visible = no
							}
							sidepanel_button_small = {
								blockoverride "icon" {
									texture = "gfx/interface/main_hud/te_systems_window_btn.dds"
								}
								blockoverride "button_selected" {
									visible = no
								}
								onclick = "[GetVariableSystem.Set('com_open_window', 'te_systems_window')]"
							}
						}
					}

					### Open: lit, and closes it.
					widget = {
						size = { 42 40 }
						visible = "[GetVariableSystem.HasValue('com_open_window', 'te_systems_window')]"

						widget = {
							using = clickthrough_blocker
							using = sidebar_button_size_small
							using = selected_sidepanel_animation_small
							blockoverride "selected_visibility" {
								visible = yes
							}
							sidepanel_button_small = {
								blockoverride "icon" {
									texture = "gfx/interface/main_hud/te_systems_window_btn.dds"
								}
								blockoverride "button_selected" {
									visible = yes
								}
								onclick = "[GetVariableSystem.Clear('com_open_window')]"
							}
						}
					}

					sidebar_tooltip_area_small = {
						tooltip = "te_systems_window_launcher_tt"
					}
				}
			}
		}
	}
}

### ==========================================================================
### THE WINDOW (registered: te_systems_window). Where vanilla's side panels
### sit: a full-size root whose vbox starts under the top bar (margin_top = 85,
### as ingame_hud.gui lays out its panels; demography_cmf_panel.gui is the
### shipping precedent), and default_block_window brings the sidebar margin.
### The root is up only while the window is open, and its own _show closes
### whatever vanilla side panel or ledger is open: the root's visibility is
### what flips when the window opens.
### ==========================================================================
widget = {
	name = "te_systems_window"
	size = { 100% 100% }
	layer = layer_ingame_menu
	visible = "[And( And( GetMetaPlayer.GetPlayedOrObservedCountry.IsValid, Not( GetMetaPlayer.IsObserver ) ), GetVariableSystem.HasValue('com_open_window', 'te_systems_window') )]"

	state = {
		name = _show
		on_start = "[InformationPanelBar.ClosePanel]"
		on_start = "[MapListPanelManager.CloseCurrentPanel]"
	}

	### The side panel: where vanilla's side panels sit, while
	### te_systems_window_wide is unset (the toggle; client-side, never saved).
	widget = {
		size = { 100% 100% }
		visible = "[Not( GetVariableSystem.Exists('te_systems_window_wide') )]"

		vbox = {
			parentanchor = top|left
			layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
			layoutpolicy_vertical = expanding
			margin_top = 85

			widget = {
				layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
				layoutpolicy_vertical = expanding

				hbox = {
					layoutpolicy_horizontal = expanding
					layoutpolicy_vertical = expanding

					te_systems_window_panel = {}
					expand = {}
				}
			}
		}
	}

	### The full screen, while it is set: at the root's origin, full size, as
	### vanilla's full-screen panels sit in ingame_hud.gui. The frame draws its
	### own top filler, which the top bar draws over, and starts its content
	### below it (margin_top = 100), so no inset. The HUD's layer is above this
	### one, so the top bar and the sidebar draw over the frame
	### (docs/testing/te-window-wide-playtest.md, item 1). What vanilla hides
	### under its own full screens (the outliner, the "Paused" frame, the lens
	### toolbar, the construction box) hides under this one too: the three
	### templates in gui/00_te_fullscreen_hide.gui read this visible and the
	### root's (test_te_fullscreen_hud.py), so change them with either.
	te_systems_window_panel_wide = {
		visible = "[GetVariableSystem.Exists('te_systems_window_wide')]"
	}
}
